**Approach.** Thread A prints the odd vector's elements, thread B prints the even vector's. They must alternate, so use a shared `turn` flag protected by a mutex, with a condition variable that each thread waits on until it is its turn.

    #include <iostream>
    #include <vector>
    #include <thread>
    #include <mutex>
    #include <condition_variable>

    std::mutex m;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    bool oddTurn = true;

    void worker(const std::vector<int>& v, bool isOdd) {
        for (int x : v) {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);
            cv.wait(lock, [&] { return oddTurn == isOdd; });
            std::cout << x << ' ';
            oddTurn = !oddTurn;
            cv.notify_all();
        }
    }

    int main() {
        std::vector<int> odd{1,3,5,7,9}, even{2,4,6,8,10};
        std::thread a(worker, std::cref(odd), true);
        std::thread b(worker, std::cref(even), false);
        a.join(); b.join();
    }

**Why it works.** Each thread waits until `oddTurn` matches its role, prints, flips the flag and wakes the other. The predicate form of `wait` protects against spurious wakeups and lost notifications. Output is `1 2 3 ... 10`. Include `<functional>` for `std::cref`. This works as long as both vectors have matching lengths; for unequal lengths a thread would wait forever once the other finishes, so add a "done" condition.

Alternatives are two semaphores (C++20 `std::binary_semaphore`), one released by each thread for the other, which is the same idea with less shared state.

**Other topics in the round.**

- **`const_cast`** removes or adds `const` or `volatile` on a pointer or reference. Modifying an object that was originally declared `const` through it is undefined behaviour; it is meant for calling legacy APIs that are not const-correct.
- **Virtual inheritance** solves the diamond problem: with `class B : virtual public A` and `class C : virtual public A`, a class `D : B, C` contains only one shared `A` subobject instead of two.
- **Pages** are fixed-size blocks (commonly 4 KB) of virtual memory. The OS maps virtual pages to physical frames via page tables and loads pages on demand, causing page faults when a page is not in memory.
